 "findings": [
  "The mechanism works. Mean peak part lineage age rose from 18.1 generations to 32.5, and at minute 90 the oldest surviving part averaged 19.9 generations against 9.9 in the control. Structure genuinely stops churning.",
  "Depth survives much better. Bodies at least two parts deep were standing at minute 90 in seven of eight entrenched seeds against four of eight controls, and mean bodied share across the run rose from 31.6 percent to 44.6 percent.",
  "It is not a museum. The risk named in the design document was that entrenched lineages would persist while populations sickened. They did not: mean population over the run was 26.2 with the lever against 25.3 without, and 24.1 against 21.0 at minute 90. Holding structure cost these worlds nothing measurable in health.",
  "Eyes are the bottleneck. Any eyed creature at all was standing at minute 90 in exactly two of eight seeds in BOTH arms. Entrenchment keeps a body once built; it gives nothing a reason to build or keep an eye, and the complete animal criterion is gated on eyes.",
  "The collapse really is a ratchet giving way. Seed 1006 with the lever on reached minute 84 with 31 of 51 creatures bodied and its oldest part 75 generations deep, then arrived at minute 90 with 5 bodied and its oldest part 2 generations deep: a deeply entrenched population lost its structure wholesale inside one six minute sample. The baseline replay of 60606 shows the same shape, mean part age halving from 58 to 24 across the window where eyes fell from 14 to 2. Long held structure is not lost gradually as a cost benefit reversal would predict; it goes all at once, and entrenchment slows the ratchet without fitting it with a pawl.",
  "Entrenchment can actively harm a world. Seed 60606 ended the entrenched arm with no bodied creatures at all and a population of 11, where its own control ended with 40 bodied of 41. Reducing the shed rate early appears able to lock a lineage into a configuration it then cannot leave."
 ],
 "honestLimitations": [
  "ENTRENCH_HALF=20 was chosen by judgement and never calibrated. The prewritten test is reported as it stands, and it failed at that setting; a half life sweep is a legitimate follow up and would distinguish a wrong mechanism from a wrong dial.",
  "Eight paired seeds on one machine, ninety minutes each. The paired design controls seed quality but the sample is small, and several of the secondary effects rest on one or two seeds.",
  "The cross machine divergence above means these numbers describe this machine's worlds. The paired comparison is unaffected, since both arms ran here."
 ],
 "shipped": {
  "state": "Instrumentation ships live; the lever ships dormant at ENTRENCH=0, which is bit for bit v1.39.2.",
  "rationale": "Design requirement 7 asks that a lever be reversible to neutral by a dial, and requirement 8 that its release note report the study result even when it fails. Both are met. The instrumentation is kept because it produced the finding and costs nothing.",
  "persisted": "la and oa are saved as elements 7 and 8 of each part's array. Saves written before v1.40 are seven long and load with both clocks at zero, which is the neutral default."
 },
 "forTheRoadmap": "Necessity moves to the front of the persistence queue. The eye, not the branch, is what worlds cannot hold, and nothing in the current economy pays a lineage to keep one. That is exactly what Lever 1 was for, and the Garden is the version of it that does not read as a script.",
